digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N12" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If pre-gating is essential but no automated methods exist for it, and existing methods still require manual pre-gating, then there is a critical gap in automation."];
  "N13" [label="(21,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: The goal is to automate the analysis process."];
  "N14" [label="(22,1,23) Abduction-knowledge: Deep learning offers a powerful computational approach for semi-automated gating of cytometry data."];
  "N17" [label="(25,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: A major challenge in automatic pre-gating is technical and biological variance across subjects."];
  "N19" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If there is variance in data that causes fluctuations in measurements, then methods must be robust to positional and intensity variations."];
  "N2" [label="(10,0,0) Manually defining boundaries for each cell subtype and sample is time-consuming and labor-intensive."];
  "N20" [label="(27,0,0) Deduction-case: Only predicting terminal cell types prevents accurate gating of intermediate cell types."];
  "N23" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If hierarchical structure provides interpretability and captures intermediate cell types, then automated methods should adopt hierarchical approaches."];
  "N25" [label="(34,0,0) Induction-case: Convolutional architecture was originally designed for image classification tasks."];
  "N26" [label="(35,0,0) Induction-case: Convolutional architecture can detect target objects regardless of position and learn general features."];
  "N27" [label="(36,0,0) Induction-case: This versatility led to adaptation for image segmentation tasks."];
  "N28" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: If convolutional networks handle positional variance in images and perform segmentation, then they can be applied to similar spatial data problems."];
  "N29" [label="(37,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Manual gating is challenging for automated software because human experts have a global view of cell density in protein measurement spaces."];
  "N30" [label="(7,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: Gating is defined as boundaries on bivariate density maps of cell populations."];
  "N36" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Convolutional networks handle images with positional variance (N25, N26, N27), and they can be applied to similar spatial problems (N28). Therefore, convolutional networks can handle spatial variance in cytometric data."];
  "N37" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Manual gating requires global view of density (N29), and gating uses bivariate density maps (N30). Therefore, automated gating should work with density map representations."];
  "N38" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-case: Convolutional networks can handle variance (N36), and automated gating needs density map representation (N37)."];
  "N4" [label="(12,0,0) Deduction-case: Unsupervised methods often produce unrecognizable cell clusters and fail to capture rare cell types, making interpretation challenging."];
  "N41" [label="(31,0,0) Deduction-case: The strategy for manual pre-gating can be extended to automatic gating since cell-type gates have more stable boundaries than pre-gates."];
  "N43" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If pre-gating and gating can be unified under the same strategy, then a single framework can address both tasks."];
  "N51" [label="(41,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: UNITO can learn pre-gating and gating tasks from human annotation and adaptively process independent data."];
  "N52" [label="(42,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: The ability to perform inference on unseen samples without supervision enables large-scale immunology studies."];
  "N53" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: UNITO learns from human annotation and processes independent data (N51), and unsupervised inference enables large-scale studies (N52). Therefore, UNITO enables scalable automated cytometric analysis."];
  "N55" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If manual processes are labor-intensive and automated alternatives exist, then automation should be pursued."];
  "N56" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If convolutional networks handle spatial variance and gating needs density maps, then convolutional networks can be applied to density map-based gating."];
  "N6" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If automated methods cannot reproduce expert gating and produce uninterpretable results, then they are inadequate for practical cytometric analysis."];
  "N60" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If convolutional networks handle variance and density maps, and a unified solution is needed, then an image segmentation approach using convolutional networks on density maps can provide unified automated gating."];
  "N7" [label="(15,0,0) Deduction-case: Methods typically assume single cells have been separated from doublets and debris (pre-gating)."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate UNITO as an automated cytometric gating framework that converts bivariate protein-expression data into density images and applies convolutional image-segmentation networks to learn expert-annotated hierarchical pre-gating and gating contours, enabling adaptive identification of cell populations across cytometry modalities.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When cytometric gating and pre-gating have unmet automation needs, and a method can represent bivariate protein-expression data as density images for convolutional image segmentation while learning expert hierarchical gating contours, the combined evidence supports developing and evaluating an automated cytometric gating framework."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Convolutional networks can be applied to density-map-based cytometric gating when spatial-variance handling is paired with the need for density-map representations."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) There is a critical automation gap in pre-gating because pre-gating is assumed or required by current workflows, remains labor-intensive, and lacks efficient adaptive open-source automation."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Automated deep-learning approaches for cytometry should incorporate hierarchical gating structure to preserve intermediate cell populations and interpretability."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) A single automated framework can address both pre-gating and gating when these tasks are treated under a shared gating strategy."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Labor-intensive manual gating in cytometric analysis motivates pursuit of automated gating approaches."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Automated gating solutions should be pursued because manual gating is labor-intensive and automated gating alternatives have been developed."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Automated pre-gating methods need robustness to positional and intensity variation caused by technical and biological variance across samples."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) A new deep-learning approach for cytometric automation should be investigated to address limitations of existing deep-learning methods while supporting hierarchical gating."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) The need for unified automated gating, variance robustness, and density-map-compatible convolutional networks motivates a variance-robust deep-learning approach on density maps."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because cytometric analysis is intended to be automated and deep learning offers computational approaches for semi-automated cytometry gating, deep learning is a reasonable approach to investigate for automation."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Existing unsupervised automated gating methods are inadequate for practical cytometric analysis when they produce uninterpretable clusters, miss rare cell types, and fail to reproduce expert gating."];
  "N29" -> "N37" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "N30" -> "N37" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "N51" -> "N53" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
